   "source": [
    "## Homework 4 - Analysis Using Hotelling's $T^2$ and Multiple Stream Process Control Charts\n",
    "### Dr. Austin Brown\n",
    "### Kennesaw State University\n",
    "\n", Takaylah Bonds 
    "**DUE: November 20, 2026 via D2L**\n",
    "\n",
    "## Instructions: \n",
    "\n",
    "You are the director of The Owl's Nest, a psychological services program at a local university that employs a team of four Clinical Psychology PhD students to offer counseling services to the campus community at a steep discount. When a new client comes in, they commit to biweekly sessions for at least one semester. At the start of each session, the client completes a brief survey assessing depressive symptoms using Likert-scale items (e.g., \"How much do you agree with this statement, on a scale from 1 (strongly disagree) to 5 (strongly agree)?\"). The total score ranges from 20 to 100, with higher scores indicating more severe depressive symptoms.\n",
    "\n",
    "If a counselor's client scores begin trending upward over time, that may indicate a need for you, as the director, to step in -- whether by providing additional training and supervision, or by referring a client to a higher level of care. Because all four counselors work in the same training program, share office space and supervision, and often see clients during the same high-stress points in the academic calendar (midterms, finals), you suspect their clients' scores may be correlated with one another from week to week, rather than behaving as four completely independent processes.\n",
    "\n",
    "To monitor this, you perform an initial Phase I analysis, jointly monitoring the scores of all four counselors across the 16-week fall semester, taking a sample of 5 clients per week for each counselor. After confirming statistical control, you'll monitor the subsequent 16-week spring semester (Phase II) to determine whether anything has changed.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Key Specifications\n",
    "\n",
    "| Quantity | Value |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Quality characteristic | Total depressive-symptom survey score (20-100 scale) |\n",
    "| Number of counselors (streams) | 4 |\n",
    "| Clients sampled per counselor per week (n) | 5 |\n",
    "| Phase I weeks (m) | 16 (fall semester) |\n",
    "| Phase II weeks (m) | 16 (spring semester) |\n",
    "| Control Chart for Each Stream design value | $L = 3.46$ |"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fa85ebf7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Define & Measure\n",
    "\n",
    "### Q1: Briefly describe the purpose of this analysis\n",
    "\n",The purpose of this analysis is to monitor depressive symptom scores across four counselors at The Owl's Nest to determine whether the counseling program is statistically stable. By comparing scores from the fall and spring semesters, we can identify unusual changes in depressive symptoms and determine whether additional supervision, training, or client support may be needed.
    "### Q2: Identify the quality characteristic being monitored, and classify its measurement scale. Why might it be reasonable to suspect that the four counselors' weekly scores are correlated with one another, rather than fully independent?\n",
    "\n",The four counselors' scores may be correlated because they work in the same environment, receive similar supervision, and serve students experiencing common academic stressors, such as midterms and final exams. These shared factors may cause depressive symptom scores to change together.
    "### Q3: Given your answer to Q2, briefly explain why it might be reasonable to treat this scenario as a **multivariate process** (suggesting a Hotelling's $T^2$ chart) rather than a **multiple stream process** (suggesting the Control Chart for Each Stream technique). Under what different assumption would the opposite framing make more sense?\n",
    "\n",A multivariate process is reasonable because the four counselors' scores may be correlated due to shared environmental and academic factors. A Hotelling's \(T^2\) chart accounts for relationships between the counselors' scores and monitors them together.
    "## Analyze\n",
    "\n",
    "### Q4: Read the Phase I data (`Counselling_PhaseI.xlsx`) into R as a list, one data frame per counselor (HINT: use `qcc::mqcc`, and make sure to remove the `Week` column from each data frame, possibly using `lapply`). Construct a Phase I Hotelling's $T^2$ chart. Do we have evidence of statistical control? Why or why not?"
   ]
  },The Phase I data show evidence of statistical control. The weekly scores for all four counselors remain within the calculated within the control limits. This suggests the counseling program was statistically stable during the fall semester and provides a reasonable baseline for monitoring the spring semester.
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "aee2b9e1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "## Q4 Code ##"
   ]
  },

library(readxl)
library(qcc)

# Read the four counselor worksheets
phase1 <- lapply(1:4, function(i) {
  as.data.frame(
    read_excel("Counselling_PhaseI.xlsx", sheet = i)
  )
})

# Remove Week column
phase1 <- lapply(phase1, function(x) {
  x[, -1]
})

# Name the counselors
names(phase1) <- paste0("Counselor", 1:4)

# Construct Phase I Hotelling's T2 chart
phase1_chart <- mqcc(
  phase1,
  type = "T2",
  confidence.level = 0.9973
)

summary(phase1_chart)

