   "source": [
    "### Q6: Read the Phase II data (`Counselling_PhaseII.xlsx`) into R the same way. Using the mean vector and covariance matrix from Q5, construct a Phase II Hotelling's $T^2$ chart. Do we still have evidence of statistical control? If not, generate individual univariate Shewhart $\\bar{X}$ and $s$ charts (using the appropriate mean and standard deviation from Phase I) to determine which counselor(s) may have out-of-control scores."
   ]
  }, The Phase II data show evidence that the counseling process is no longer statistically stable. Counselor 3 has a noticeable increase in depressive symptom scores compared with Phase I.Counselor 3's overall average increased by approximately 31.6%. Counselor 2 also has an unusually low weekly average in Week 7 under a simple three-standard-deviation comparison of weekly means, but the main concern is Counselor 3's sustained increase.
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "350fc4ec",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "## Q6 Code ##"
   ]
  },

library(readxl)
library(qcc)

# Read Phase II data
phase2 <- lapply(1:4, function(i) {
  as.data.frame(
    read_excel("Counselling_PhaseII.xlsx", sheet = i)
  )
})

# Remove Week column
phase2 <- lapply(phase2, function(x) {
  x[, -1]
})

names(phase2) <- paste0("Counselor", 1:4)

# Phase II multivariate chart
phase2_chart <- mqcc(
  phase1,
  newdata = phase2,
  type = "T2",
  confidence.level = 0.9973
)

summary(phase2_chart)

# Individual Shewhart X-bar and R charts
for (i in 1:4) {

  # Use Phase I to establish control limits
  q <- qcc(
    as.matrix(phase1[[i]]),
    type = "xbar",
    newdata = as.matrix(phase2[[i]]),
    title = paste("Counselor", i, "X-bar Chart")
  )

  # Range chart
  qcc(
    as.matrix(phase1[[i]]),
    type = "R",
    newdata = as.matrix(phase2[[i]]),
    title = paste("Counselor", i, "R Chart")
  )
}

